>

  <!-- § 01 the zero mark -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The zero mark</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Read any Indian statistic closely and a year is attached to it. The consumer price index is published on base 2012 = 100. GDP is measured at constant 2011–12 prices. The year is not decoration. It is the reference point every other reading is measured against, and the source of the weights that decide what matters in the number.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Base year <span class="sub">the zero mark on the ruler</span></div>
          <p>The reference period of an index or a constant-price series. For a price index, the basket's cost in the base year is set to 100 and every other month is measured against it. For GDP, the base year's prices are the fixed yardstick that turns rupees into output.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> not a claim that things were normal then; just a place to stand the ruler.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">The base year also supplies the weights. The CPI basket's shares, food at a little under half of household spending, housing at about a tenth, come from the consumption survey of 2011–12. Those shares are a photograph of how India spent in the base year, frozen into the index. Every print since is that old photograph repriced.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Base revision <span class="sub">moving the zero · reweighing the basket</span></div>
          <p>The periodic rebuilding of a series on a newer reference year. The basket is reweighed from a fresh survey, the item list is updated, and the index is re-anchored at 100 in the new year. GDP gets new prices, fresh surveys of the unorganised sector, and sometimes new methods.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the ruler is re-marked so it measures the India that exists, not the one that did.</div>
        </aside>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · i</span>
          <b>The year is furniture.</b> 2012 = 100 says where the ruler starts. It says nothing about whether 2012 was good, bad or normal.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · ii</span>
          <b>Weights are a photograph.</b> The basket's shares are frozen from a survey of the base year. Every print is that photograph repriced.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iii</span>
          <b>Roughly once a decade.</b> The CPI moved to base 2012 and GDP to base 2011–12 in 2015. Re-anchoring is a matter of when, not whether.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 why bases grow old -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>Why bases grow old</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Drift, slowly and one-way</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>A base begins to age the moment it is set. The 2011–12 spending survey predates cheap mobile data, food delivery, smartphones in every pocket and the goods-and-services tax. An index weighted on that India undercounts what the present India buys and overweights what it has stopped buying. The drift is slow, but it runs one way: the ruler stays calibrated to a country that no longer quite exists.</p>
        <p>So bases are revised, roughly once a decade. In 2015 the CPI was re-anchored to 2012 and GDP to 2011–12, with new weights, new items and new surveys underneath. The exercise is maintenance, not makeover. A statistic that never changed its base would grow more precise and less true with every passing year.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · iv</span>
          <b>Precision is not truth.</b> An index can be computed to two decimals on weights a decade out of date. The decimals survive; the relevance does not.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 what changes -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>What a revision changes, and what it cannot</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">The map, not the territory</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>A new base changes the weights, the levels and sometimes the growth story. When GDP moved to the 2011–12 base in 2015, growth rates for years already past were restated, and some of them rose on paper without a single extra factory being built. That is not forgery. It is the same past measured with a better ruler, and the honest way to handle it is to publish the new series and say what changed.</p>
        <p>What a revision cannot change is the past itself. The wheat harvested in 2012 does not grow because the ruler moved. What changes is the measurement: which goods count, at what weights, at whose prices. A base revision is a better map of the same territory. The old map was not a lie. It was old.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note · v</span>
          <b>The past happens once.</b> Our estimate of it keeps improving. A restated growth rate is the measurement catching up, not history being relived.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“A new base year is the government moving the goalposts.”</h3>
          <p>Base revisions happen under governments of every party, on roughly a ten-year cycle, because the economy changes under all of them. The methods are published and the new weights are public. Judge a revision by its stated method, not by whether you preferred the old number.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“The base year is when things were normal.”</h3>
          <p>No normality is implied. 2012 is not a golden age; it is a zero mark. The index would work exactly as well anchored to any other year, because a base is a convention, not a verdict on the times.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Base revisions rewrite history.”</h3>
          <p>They rewrite the measurement of history. When the 2011–12 base restated earlier growth, no year was relived and no harvest grew. The ruler changed; the past did not.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. The CPI is published on base 2012 = 100 with weights from the 2011–12 consumption survey; GDP is published at constant 2011–12 prices. Both bases were adopted in 2015, replacing the earlier reference years.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Base year and base revision are used in their statistical senses: the zero mark of an index, and its periodic re-marking. The page claims no date for the next revision; bases move roughly once a decade and the timing is a matter of published decision, not of this note's forecast.</p>
    <p>Where the past was restated under a new base, this page describes the fact without adjudicating the method.</p>
  </div>
